    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Equation 1: $2x + 3y = 6$\nIsolate $y$ on one side of the equation: \n\\begin{align*}\n3y &= -2x + 6 \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\n\\end{align*}\nThe slope of Equation 1 is \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\).\n\nThe slope of a line $mx + ny = p$ is $-\\frac{n}{m}$.\n\nSo the slope of the line through the perpendicular line will be\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{slope of perpendicular line} &= -\\frac{3}{-2} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}.\n\\end{align*} \n\nWhat would the answer be if the question asked for the slope of the angle between the two lines? To find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\), we already determined that the slope is \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\). Since the slope of the perpendicular line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the original line, the slope of the perpendicular line is \\(-\\frac{3}{2}\\).\n\nNext, to find the slope of the angle between the two lines, we use the formula for the angle between two lines with slopes \\(m_1\\) and \\(m_2\\):\n\\[\n\\tan \\theta = \\left| \\frac{m_1 - m_2}{1 + m_1 m_2} \\right|\n\\]\nHere, \\(m_1 = -\\frac{2}{3}\\) (the slope of the original line) and \\(m_2 = -\\frac{3}{2}\\) (the slope of the line perpendicular to it).\n\nSo,\n\\[\n\\tan \\theta = \\left| \\frac{-\\frac{2}{3} - \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{1 + \\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{-\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{3}{2}}{1 + 1} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{\\frac{3}{6} + \\frac{9}{6}}{2} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{\\frac{12}{6}}{2} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{2}{2} \\right| = 1\n\\]\nTherefore, the slope of the angle between the two lines is 1.",
